</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Overall results (<math id="S5.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=90 per condition; <sup id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>SR uses runs 1–2 only, <math id="S5.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=60, Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>; v1 values with run 3: F1 24.5, Prec. 25.8, Rec. 24.2). Finds/TP/FP/FN are per-artifact averages; Prec./Rec./F1 in %.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CCR leads on F1 and precision; SR has slightly higher recall. The SR2 numbers tell an interesting story: the second review pass has substantially worse precision than single-pass SR (21.0% vs 26.2%). The model is generating more noise, not more signal. Figure <a href="#S5.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 5.6 Reproducibility Across Runs ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> visualizes F1 across conditions and categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Statistical Tests</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Statistical Tests ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> reports paired <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests on per-artifact F1 from Run 1 (<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=30), as in the first version; the robustness test on three-run averages is in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:82.7pt;vertical-align:-38.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(44.8pt,-10.7pt) scale(1.35125401717109,1.35125401717109) ;">
<table id="S5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comparison</span></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S5.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cohen’s</span> <math id="S5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T3.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sig.</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">SR vs CCR</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>2.849</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.008</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.52</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">Yes**</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SR2 vs CCR</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.917</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">&lt;0.001</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.72</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Yes***</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SA vs CCR</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>3.119</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.004</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.57</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Yes**</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T3.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">SR vs SR2</th>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">1.665</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.107</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="S5.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math>0.30</td>
<td id="S5.T3.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">No</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Statistical comparisons (paired <math id="S5.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-test, Run 1). <math id="S5.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as the first minus the second condition in each row; Cohen’s <math id="S5.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S5.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{z}</annotation></semantics></math>, the mean paired difference (second minus first) divided by its standard deviation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In run 1, every comparison against CCR is significant at <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> &lt; 0.01, with medium-to-large effect sizes (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.52–0.72), and SR vs SR2 is not significant (<math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.11). These run-1 results do not hold across runs for SR and SA (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Detection by Severity</h3>

<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Condition</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Critical</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Major</span></th>
<th id="S5.T4.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T4.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Minor</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCR</span></th>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T4.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">29%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">18%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SR<sup id="S5.T4.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup></th>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">32%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T4.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">32%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T4.2.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">22%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SA</th>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">30%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">23%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">16%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">SR2</th>
<td id="S5.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">31%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">26%</td>
<td id="S5.T4.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">16%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Detection rate by severity (<sup id="S5.T4.4" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>SR runs 1–2 only; v1: 29/27/19%).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CCR’s advantage over SR appears only for critical errors (40% vs 32%, an 8-point gap); SR is ahead on major (32% vs 29%) and minor (22% vs 18%) errors. These are descriptive rates without significance tests. Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.6 Reproducibility Across Runs ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates the severity-dependent pattern.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>Performance by Artifact Category</h3>

<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S5.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Category</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SA</span></th>
<th id="S5.T5.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T5.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SR2</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Code</th>
<td id="S5.T5.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T5.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">40.7%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">37.0%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">34.1%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">31.9%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Document</th>
<td id="S5.T5.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T5.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">24.5%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">22.3%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">17.4%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">19.2%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T5.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T5.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Script</th>
<td id="S5.T5.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">20.7%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T5.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">22.1%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">19.9%</td>
<td id="S5.T5.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">14.0%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>F1 by artifact category (3-run average; SR runs 1–2 only; v1 SR: 36.0/18.2/19.5%).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CCR leads on code (+3.7 F1 points over SR) and documents (+2.2), and SR leads on scripts (22.1% vs 20.7%). This makes sense: code review benefits particularly from a “fresh eyes” perspective that questions implementation assumptions rather than accepting them as given. SR2 performs notably poorly on scripts (14.0%), suggesting that repeated review within the same session is especially counterproductive for subjective content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>Detection by Error Type</h3>

<figure id="S5.T6" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S5.T6.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:81.3pt;vertical-align:-37.3pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(42.5pt,-10.0pt) scale(1.32699916418125,1.32699916418125) ;">
<table id="S5.T6.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cond.</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FACT</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CONS</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CTXT</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RCVR</span></th>
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MISS</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CCR</span></th>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">48%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">37%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">13%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">19%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">19%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SR<sup id="S5.T6.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup></th>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">50%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">35%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">18%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">18%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.3.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S5.T6.2.1.3.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">20%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SA</th>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">41%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">31%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">14%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">13%</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T6.2.1.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T6.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">SR2</th>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">40%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">31%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">16%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">12%</td>
<td id="S5.T6.2.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">14%</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 6: </span>Detection rate by error type (<math id="S5.T6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=90 per cell; <sup id="S5.T6.4" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>SR <math id="S5.T6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=60, runs 1–2; v1 SR: 44/32/12/16/17%).</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Factual errors (FACT) are the easiest to catch across all conditions, which is unsurprising — they are the most concrete. Contextual errors (CTXT) are the hardest, topping out at 18% (SR). CCR has the highest detection rate for two of five types (CONS and RCVR), and SR for the other three; the differences between CCR and SR are within 6 points on every type and were not tested.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">One anomaly: SR2 detects more CTXT errors than CCR (16% vs 13%) but has the lowest RCVR detection (12%). It appears that repeated review within the same context shifts attention between error types rather than improving detection overall — a shuffling rather than an amplification, although this is a hypothesis and we did not track individual errors across passes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Reproducibility Across Runs</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The first version stated that the ranking CCR &gt; SR <math id="S5.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math> SA &gt; SR2 holds in all three runs. That statement was not accurate: CCR ranks first in run 1 only (Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12123v2/figure1.png" id="S5.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/269;" width="476" height="269" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>F1 scores by condition and artifact category (3-run average; SR runs 1–2 only). CCR leads on code and documents; SR leads on scripts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.12123v2/figure2.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/269;" width="476" height="269" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Detection rate by error severity (SR runs 1–2 only). CCR is ahead of SR only on Critical errors (+8pp); SR is ahead on Major and Minor errors. Descriptive, not tested.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Update in Version 2</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We re-examined the per-session records of this experiment while reusing them in later work. The central SR2 result holds; two findings narrow the broader comparisons.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Provenance of SR run 3.</span> The review records were produced through the Claude Code agent tool and transcribed into structured files by the host session; they do not include raw model output. For SR run 3, 92% of the finding descriptions appear verbatim inside the script that wrote the result files, so we cannot confirm that they are unedited model output. We exclude this run; the SR values in Tables <a href="#S5.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 5.1 Overall Performance ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>–<a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.5 Detection by Error Type ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> use runs 1 and 2 (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>=60). Its F1 (19.4%) was well below SR’s other runs (25.7% and 28.6%). The other runs are unverified rather than known to be clean.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Run-to-run variation.</span> The first version tested run 1 only and stated that the ranking held in all three runs. Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> shows that CCR ranks first in run 1 only.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T7" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S6.T7.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T7.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T7.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cond.</span></th>
<th id="S6.T7.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Run 1</span></th>
<th id="S6.T7.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Run 2</span></th>
<th id="S6.T7.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S6.T7.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Run 3</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S6.T7.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T7.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">CCR</th>
<td id="S6.T7.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><span id="S6.T7.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">37.2</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">25.0</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">23.7</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T7.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SR</th>
<td id="S6.T7.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">25.7</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T7.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">28.6</span></td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">(19.4, excluded)</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T7.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SA</th>
<td id="S6.T7.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">23.8</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">20.8</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T7.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">26.9</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T7.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T7.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">SR2</th>
<td id="S6.T7.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">18.7</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">22.5</td>
<td id="S6.T7.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">23.9</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 7: </span>F1 (%) per run, averaged over 30 artifacts.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Robustness test.</span> Averaging each artifact’s F1 over its available runs and comparing with CCR by Wilcoxon signed-rank tests (Holm-adjusted over three comparisons): SR2 <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.004, SA <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.057, SR <math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.26. With SR run 3 included, all three would be significant (<math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.004, 0.043, 0.043), which is why the exclusion matters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What holds.</span> Reviewing again in the same session (SR2) is worse than reviewing in a fresh session (CCR), in the run-1 test and in the three-run average (in run 3 alone the two are level). <span id="S6.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What is narrowed.</span> Whether a fresh-session review outperforms a single self-review is not established by these data, and the per-category, per-severity, and per-type advantages over SR reported in the first version do not hold after the update. The run-1 tests in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.2 Statistical Tests ‣ 5 Results ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> are unchanged and reported for continuity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Citation corrections.</span> We also checked every citation against its source. The first version attributed to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tsui (2026)</a></cite> a rise in the blind-spot rate with task complexity (45.2% to 79.2%); these figures do not appear in that paper, which reports a 64.5% average that is consistent across model families and scales, and we have removed them. We also corrected the description of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Choi et al. (2025)</a></cite>, added a qualifier to the summary of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamoi et al. (2024)</a></cite>, and corrected author lists and publication details in the bibliography.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Why Context Separation Works</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results line up with the prediction that production context introduces systematic bias. But the SR2 result is what makes the argument tight. Reviewing twice in the same session shows no evidence of helping, and its precision is lower than single-pass SR (21.0% vs 26.2%), consistent with a second pass that adds findings rather than reconsidering the first assessment; we did not analyse the added findings. The model “tries harder” by adding noise. This is consistent with what <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. (2025)</a></cite> observed: self-correction within a fixed context can degrade initially correct judgments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The SA result adds another dimension. Knowing the generation prompt — understanding what the user originally asked for — does not improve review quality. If anything, it slightly hurts (SA F1 23.8% vs SR 27.1%; not tested). One possible reading is that the anchoring effect operates through the presence of production-related context rather than its content, but SA and SR also differ in session and review language, so this is a hypothesis. Even well-intentioned context (“here is what the user wanted”) triggers the same bias patterns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>The SR2 Result and Why It Matters</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">SR2 deserves its own subsection because it serves a critical role as a confound control.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Without SR2, a reader could reasonably attribute CCR’s advantage to a “two looks are better than one” effect. After all, CCR effectively gives the model a second chance at review, just in a different session. Maybe any second review would help, regardless of context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our data support a narrower version of this argument. A second review in the same context (SR2) does not improve on a single review (run 1, <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.11), while a second review in a fresh session (CCR) robustly outperforms SR2 (run 1 <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>&lt;0.001; three-run Holm-adjusted <math id="S7.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>=0.004). The difference between SR2 and CCR is not repetition — both involve reviewing again — but context. However, CCR does not robustly outperform a single same-session review (SR), so the data show that context separation rescues a second look, not that it beats looking once. Review language also differs between CCR (English) and SR2 (Korean), so even this comparison is not a clean isolation of context (see Limitations).</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This is a more limited claim than the first version made.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Practical Implications</h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CCR requires nothing special. No multiple models, no orchestration framework, no prompt engineering. Start a new session, paste the artifact, ask for a review. That is the entire method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This makes it immediately applicable:</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In software development, review generated code in a new session before committing.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For content creation, verify articles or documentation independently of the session that produced them.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In multi-agent pipelines, route artifacts through an independent review agent as a standard stage.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The cost overhead is small. A review session processes <math id="S7.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>5K tokens (the artifact plus the review prompt), compared to 50K+ tokens in a typical production session. In many cases, the review session is actually cheaper than continuing in the production session would be.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4 </span>Connection to Human Review Practices</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The analogy to human review practices is not decorative — it is structural. Code review, peer review, and editorial review all exist because the person who created something cannot evaluate it objectively. The creator knows too much: the intent behind ambiguous code, the reasoning behind an unusual claim, the context that makes a missing section seem acceptable. A reviewer who lacks this context is forced to engage with the artifact on its own terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CCR reproduces this dynamic for LLMs. The reviewing session does not know the intent, the reasoning, or the production context. It can only evaluate what is actually written. Our results show this to be a better starting point than a second review in the same session; whether it beats a single self-review is not established.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used a single model (Claude Opus 4.6). The theoretical motivation is model-agnostic — anchoring, context rot, and sycophancy are documented across model families — but empirical validation on GPT-4, Gemini, and open-source models would strengthen the generalizability claim.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The errors were deliberately injected, not naturally occurring. This allows precise measurement but may not reflect real-world error distributions. A preliminary case study with natural errors (supplementary Section 4A) provides some ecological validity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The absolute F1 numbers are moderate — 28.6% for the best condition. This may reflect the difficulty of the task, the matcher, or the model; we did not separate these. All conditions face the same task, so we focus on relative differences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our matching algorithm uses heuristic scoring (location proximity + keyword overlap). We validated the threshold empirically, but different matching methods would produce different absolute numbers. We did not report a threshold sensitivity analysis, and we have not repeated one for this version.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We did not run a human review baseline in the quantitative experiment. Expert human review across 30 diverse artifacts would be the ideal upper bound, but the labor cost was prohibitive for this study. Future work should include it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p6.1" class="ltx_p">There is a language confound. The artifacts and ground truth are in Korean, but the review conditions produced output in different languages: SR and SR2 reviewed in Korean (following the production session’s language), while CCR and SA reviewed in English (the default for a fresh context with no prior language cue). English-language reviews may benefit from stronger reasoning in the model’s dominant training language. We note, however, that SA also reviews in English yet underperforms CCR, so language alone does not explain the full effect.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our artifacts span three types within a single domain (software engineering tutorials in Korean). We have not tested legal documents, medical reports, or scientific manuscripts. The method should generalize — the theoretical basis applies to any context-dependent review — but this remains to be shown.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Two further limitations were identified in the audit for this version. First, the review records do not include raw model output, and one run was excluded for uncertain provenance (Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Second, the matcher’s keyword component compares findings with Korean ground-truth text, so the English-language reviews (CCR and SA) may be under-credited relative to the Korean-language ones (SR and SR2); the direction of the language confound above is therefore uncertain.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CCR as presented here is a static, single-pass method: the reviewer examines the artifact once and produces findings. Two directions extend this naturally.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, <span id="S9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">dynamic CCR</span> could incorporate multi-turn interaction between reviewer and author. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al. (2025)</a></cite> demonstrate with LLM-as-an-Interviewer that dynamic, multi-turn evaluation — where the evaluator asks follow-up questions and probes weak points — reveals failure modes that static assessment misses. Combining CCR’s context separation with interviewer-style follow-up (“Why did you choose this approach? What happens if the input is empty?”) could capture errors that a single-pass review misses while preserving the independence that makes CCR effective.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Second, <span id="S9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">hierarchical CCR</span> could scale the method beyond pairwise review. Our meta-validation (Appendix D) already shows that successive independent reviews catch qualitatively different error types. A layered architecture — workers producing artifacts, independent verifiers reviewing them, and integrators synthesizing findings — would formalize this into a systematic verification pipeline. We explore this direction in ongoing work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This research studies LLM behavior and does not involve human subjects. The artifacts are synthetic. We note that CCR, like any automated review method, can produce false positives that might mislead users. We recommend it as a supplement to human judgment, not a replacement, especially in safety-critical applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Use of AI Assistants</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The artifacts, injected errors, and all reviews were produced by Claude Opus 4.6 through the Claude Code agent tool, as described in Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Update in Version 2 ‣ Cross-Context Review: Improving LLM Output Qualityby Separating Production and Review Sessions" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. Claude Code was also used to write the analysis scripts, to recompute the numbers in this version from the per-session records, and to draft and revise text under the author’s direction. The author reviewed the final text and takes full responsibility for its content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced Cross-Context Review, a method that improves LLM verification by separating production and review into independent sessions. In a controlled experiment, a second review in a fresh session robustly outperformed a second review in the same session, in the run-1 test and in the three-run average. The first version’s broader advantages over single-pass and subagent review came from one run and are not significant across runs. Within this setting (one model, synthetic Korean artifacts, planted errors), the practical message is narrower: if a second review is wanted, a fresh session is the better place for it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The method is simple to adopt: copy the output into a new session and ask for a review there.</p>
</div>
</section>
